The first time, I chose cricket. The second time, cricket chose me. 🏏

The first time I chose cricket, I was just a kid who fell in love with the game.

I still remember walking past a cricket shop and seeing the bats displayed there. I used to look at them with so much emotion and excitement. For me, those bats were not just pieces of wood — they were dreams.

I was a huge fan of Sachin Tendulkar. I used to run from school whenever Sachin was playing. Watching him bat was something magical for me. Every shot, every movement, every moment at the crease felt special.

I used to copy everything about him — his bat grip, his walk towards the crease, his cover drive, straight drive, on-drive, cut shot, backfoot punch, and his famous leg glance. For me, he was not just a player; he was the reason I wanted to pick up a bat.

Those days, I did not even have proper cricket pads. But my love for the game was bigger than what I had. Just like the movie Lagaan, I created my own cricket pads with whatever I could arrange. And surprisingly, they turned out so good that people used to ask me, “Where did you buy these?”

For me, those questions were not just questions — they were compliments. Those small moments gave me so much happiness.

In my village, I was known as a good batsman. In a fun way, my friends started calling me “Ponting.” That name still brings a smile to my face because, at that time, I genuinely felt like a professional cricketer.

Those were probably the most beautiful days of my life. I did not have much money, but when I entered the ground with my bat in hand, I felt like a star player.

On match days, my uncle would even take leave from work just to watch me bat. That support meant everything.

I still remember tournament days. I could not sleep properly the night before because I was waiting for the morning match. The excitement was different. My mother could understand everything just by looking at my face — she knew which day I had scored well and which day I had not.

The ground was my stadium. My bat was my confidence. And cricket was my world.

But like everyone, life asked me to make choices. I had to choose my career and move ahead. With a heavy heart, I left cricket behind.

But some love stories never really end.

And maybe years later, cricket came back into my life — because the second time, cricket chose me.

=======

The second time, cricket chose me.

In 2023, I was just walking in the park when someone I did not know came to me and asked, “Do you want to play cricket?”

They were building a new society cricket team.

At that time, after almost 15 years of corporate life, with limited friends and many ups and downs of life, cricket was no longer a part of my routine. I was not very excited to start again. I only said yes because I thought it would be a good way to exercise and spend some time outside.

But I did not know that one simple “yes” would bring cricket back into my life.

I started playing weekend matches. Slowly, something changed. The same excitement that I felt as a child started coming back. I started enjoying the game again. I started waiting for weekends.

This time, cricket gave me something different. It gave me new friendships with people who were strangers at the beginning. It gave me happiness, energy, and moments to look forward to after a busy week.

The first time, I played cricket with dreams but without resources. I created my own pads because I could not buy them.

The second time, I bought my cricket pads and bats with my own money. It was a different feeling — not because of the price, but because it showed how far life had taken me.

The same love for cricket was still there. The same child was still inside me.

Only the player had changed.

This time, I was not chasing a dream of becoming someone.

I was simply enjoying the game.

But life has its own plans.

The situation in my life has not allowed me to continue this journey. Once again, I have to make a choice.

Many times, I asked myself — “Why am I taking cricket so seriously? It is just a game.”

But every time I entered the ground, I got my answer.

It was not just a game for me.

It was a place where I felt happy. A place where I forgot stress. A place where I felt relaxed and alive.

But every happiness comes with a cost.

Cricket gave me so much happiness, but somewhere along this journey, it started taking away something that I cannot afford to lose.

And now I am standing at the same place where life is asking me to choose one.

And once again, I have no other choice.

It hurts, because leaving something you love is never easy.

But I am grateful.

Grateful for the child who chose cricket the first time.

Grateful for the man who got another chance when cricket chose him the second time.

And who knows...

Maybe one day cricket will come into my life for the third time.

Only God knows. 🏏❤️


